      "latency_ms": 19837.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 28,
      "completion_tokens": 1214,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant intermolecular force is responsible for almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States (Phases)\nWater is one of the few substances that exists naturally on Earth in all three states:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Freezes at **0°C (32°F)** at standard pressure. Unusually, ice is **less dense than liquid water** (it expands ~9%), causing it to float. This insulates bodies of water, allowing life to survive underneath.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** Exists between 0°C and 100°C (32°F–212°F). This is the state most conducive to life.\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Boils at **100°C (212°F)** at standard pressure. Water vapor is a potent greenhouse gas and drives the global weather engine.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Anomalous & Critical Properties\nWater behaves differently than almost any other liquid due to hydrogen bonding:\n\n| Property | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Universal Solvent** | Dissolves more substances than any other liquid (salts, sugars, gases, acids). This allows it to transport nutrients in cells and minerals in geology. |\n| **High Specific Heat Capacity** | Absorbs a lot of heat before temperature rises. **Regulates Earth’s climate** and stabilizes internal body temperatures of organisms. |\n| **High Heat of Vaporization** | Requires massive energy to evaporate. **Cooling mechanism** for sweating (animals) and transpiration (plants). |\n| **Cohesion & Adhesion** | Sticks to itself (cohesion = surface tension) and other surfaces (adhesion = capillary action). Allows water to move up tall trees against gravity. |\n| **Density Anomaly** | Maximum density at **4°C**. Lakes freeze from the top down, preserving aquatic life in winter. |\n| **Amphoteric Nature** | Acts as both an acid (donates H⁺) and a base (accepts H⁺). Crucial for biochemical reactions and pH buffering. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role\n*   **The Medium of Life:** All known biochemical reactions occur in aqueous solution.\n*   **Metabolism:** Participates directly in **hydrolysis** (breaking bonds using water) and **condensation/dehydration synthesis** (building bonds by removing water).\n*   **Photosynthesis:** The electron donor in oxygenic photosynthesis ($6CO_2 + 6H_2O \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6 + 6O_2$).\n*   **Structure:** Provides **turgor pressure** in plant cells (rigidity) and acts as a hydrostatic skeleton in soft-bodied animals (worms, jellyfish).\n*   **Transport:** Blood (mostly water) transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, and waste.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Context (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **Coverage:** ~71% of Earth's surface.\n*   **Distribution:**\n    *   **97.5% Saline** (Oceans).\n    *   **2.5% Freshwater** (mostly locked in ice caps/glaciers ~68.7%, groundwater ~30.1%, surface water/lakes/rivers ~0.3%, atmosphere ~0.001%).\n*   **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Continuous movement driven by solar energy: Evaporation → Transpiration → Condensation → Precipitation → Infiltration/Runoff.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Use & Significance\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawal (irrigation).\n*   **Industry:** ~19% (cooling, processing, manufacturing).\n*   **Domestic:** ~11% (drinking, sanitation, hygiene).\n*   **Energy:** Hydroelectric power; cooling for thermal/nuclear plants.\n*   **Transport:** Shipping routes (oceans, rivers, canals).\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water (H₂O) is a small, polar, inorganic molecule whose hydrogen bonding network creates anomalous physical properties—most notably a solid phase less dense than its liquid phase, high thermal stability, and unparalleled solvent capacity. These properties make it the indispensable solvent, reactant, temperature buffer, and transport medium for planetary climate regulation and all known biology.**"
